236	<section id="question">
237	  <h2><span class="num">1</span>The question and what ran</h2>
238	  <ul>
239	    <li><b>The question.</b> What workflow would a designer build for batch 10 from its goals alone, and where would it spend less than ours? In the brief's words: "to see what you would design from the goals alone" <span class="src">Brief</span>.</li>
240	    <li><b>What ran first.</b> Two design-only workers, one on Opus and one on Fable, each meant to be blind to our pipeline and to the other <span class="src">Held</span>. Section 2 shows they were only partly blind.</li>
241	    <li><b>Then a blind rerun</b>, at your word ("Yes, run it. Blind rerun. Go."): two new designers, one on Opus and one on Fable, from a brief that says only what batch 10 must achieve. Section 3 says how <span class="src">Study</span>.</li>
242	    <li><b>What each wrote.</b> One document: the design, its Workflow script, how it checks correctness, its cost estimate, and what to measure or decide first <span class="src">Brief</span>.</li>
243	    <li><b>Nothing was built, run or tested.</b> All four designs exist on paper only.</li>
244	    <li><b>Batch 10 runs now on the existing pipeline</b>, launched at 05:27 UTC <span class="src">Held</span>, so its measured cost can be set beside all four designs once it lands.</li>
245	  </ul>
246	</section>
247	
248	<!-- 2 -->
249	<section id="brief">
250	  <h2><span class="num">2</span>How the first designers were briefed</h2>
251	  <p class="gist">They got the goals, your binding rules, the machine's costs and a general scripting reference. They did not get our pipeline, but parts of it showed through.</p>
252	
253	  <div class="sub">
254	    <h3>What they were given</h3>
255	    <ul>
256	      <li><b>Batch 10's goals</b>: the record's sections 1 to 5 <span class="src">Brief</span>. These describe four <i>rungs</i>. A rung is one piece of work, done by one worker in its own copy of the tree. W changes walk's (the interpreter's) load-time checks; C repairs the compiled checker's defects; G and N repair one-off slips in two parts of the library <span class="src">Rec §1</span>.</li>
257	      <li><b>Your requirements</b> from POSITIONS, as binding <span class="src">Brief</span>:
258	        <ul>
259	          <li>test first, the test kept in the corpus;</li>
260	          <li>the suite's verdict is the check, with no output comparisons;</li>
261	          <li>nothing built or run twice on the same code;</li>
262	          <li>both full suites green on the merged tree before anything lands (<code>testFast</code>, which holds the compiler tests, and <code>testSystem</code>, the interpreter tests);</li>
263	          <li>workers commit only what <code>main</code> is to hold;</li>
264	          <li>the specification revised with each change in the S1 form (a marked callout at the passage and an Appendix I entry with the reason);</li>
265	          <li>tiers: Opus for workers, Sonnet for archaeology (reading history, transcripts and logs), Fable, the top tier with its own scarcer weekly pool, only for judgements;</li>
266	          <li>costs in tokens written.</li>
267	        </ul>
268	      </li>
269	      <li><b>The machine's costs</b> <span class="src">Brief</span>: 4 CPUs; two agents at a time; about 45K tokens before an agent does anything; a prompt cache that lives 5 minutes, so a longer wait rewrites the agent's whole context; a built tree copied into a new worktree in about 3 s; the session stopped after about 13 hours; past batches wrote about 6M to 8M each.</li>
270	      <li><b>The <code>workflow-authoring</code> skill</b>, described next.</li>
271	    </ul>
272	  </div>
273	
274	  <div class="sub">
275	    <h3>The workflow-authoring skill</h3>
276	    <ul>
277	      <li>It is a reference for writing Workflow scripts: how agents are started, chained and run in parallel, how a stopped run resumes, and the traps.</li>
278	      <li>It also lists general quality patterns: several skeptics trying to refute a finding, a panel of judges, searching until a round finds nothing new, a last critic asking what is missing.</li>
279	      <li>It says nothing about Fortress, this project or its pipeline.</li>
280	      <li>It advises leaving each agent's tier unset. Both designs set a tier on every agent because the brief told them to.</li>
281	    </ul>
282	  </div>
283	
284	  <div class="sub">
285	    <h3>Choices that came from the brief, not from the designers</h3>
286	    <ul>
287	      <li>The tiers: Opus for every worker and checker, Fable only for rulings, Sonnet only for archaeology. The Opus design uses Sonnet for a cost measurement. The Fable design stretches Sonnet to the gate and the landing, and asks you first <span class="src">Fable §5</span>.</li>
288	      <li>Test first, the test committed alone, and checked by reading the worker's transcript.</li>
289	      <li>The two full suites run once on the merged tree before landing.</li>
290	      <li>One base build before the launch, every worktree seeded from it in about 3 s <span class="src">Brief</span>, and the old code run from that base build.</li>
291	      <li>Every long wait polled in steps under 5 minutes, because of the cache: 270 s in the Fable design <span class="src">Fable §2</span>, 260 s in the Opus design <span class="src">Opus §1</span>.</li>
292	      <li>The longest rung started first, because only two agents run at a time.</li>
293	      <li>A run stopped at 13 hours is resumed from its journal.</li>
294	    </ul>
295	    <p>What is left as the designers' own: how many checkers, when a ruling happens and on which tier, and what runs after the rungs.</p>
296	  </div>
297	
298	  <div class="sub">
299	    <h3>How blind they were: partly</h3>
300	    <ul>
301	      <li><b>Withheld:</b> the pipeline script, its manual, the record's sections 6 to 8, the batch reviews and the post-mortems <span class="src">Brief</span>.</li>
302	      <li><b>But section 1 of the record ends with "Cost"</b>: batch 9's cost by stage (workers 2.69M, first skeptics 1.24M, two refusal chains 1.40M, the tail 1.56M) and the old pipeline's estimate for batch 10, 6M to 7M <span class="src">Rec §1</span>. Both designs measure their savings against these numbers.</li>
303	      <li>Section 1 also defines a rung, the skeptic and the gate. Section 4 names the gather and the order it merges in <span class="src">Rec §1, §4</span>.</li>
304	      <li><b>Section 3's rung texts carry lines of the existing pipeline</b>: each rung's "For the skeptic" paragraph and its gate lines. The brief said so and asked the designers to read them as the old way <span class="src">Brief</span>.</li>
305	      <li><b>POSITIONS describes parts of it</b>: the shared base build and a skeptic that reads instead of rebuilding ("Nothing is built or run twice on the same code."), the merged-diff review beside the gate ("A blocking second review does not hold a green batch."), and Opus for a judge's first ruling, Fable for a second ("The judge's rulings.") <span class="src">POSITIONS</span>.</li>
306	      <li><code>explorations/coordinator/build-cache-exploration.md</code>, which the brief pointed to, describes batch 8's skeptics, second skeptics and second gate in its section 4.</li>
307	      <li><b>The Opus designer declares a slip.</b> The file viewer returned the record in two pages, and it read the second, sections 6 to 8. It marks what it took from them with [6-8]: the reuse of the base build, the briefings' sizes and the worker time guesses <span class="src">Opus header</span>.</li>
308	    </ul>
309	    <p>So both designers knew the pipeline's shape in part: worker, skeptic, one repair, merge, gate. The experiment shows best what each would change in that shape, not what each would invent from nothing.</p>
310	  </div>
311	</section>
