354	    <h3>Tiers: the designers' own choice</h3>
355	    <ul>
356	      <li>No rule told them which tier runs what. Both chose a mix of all three.</li>
357	      <li><b>Blind Fable</b> put Fable on the three implementers whose edit restates a rule every test depends on, Opus on judgement over much reading, Sonnet on everything mechanical <span class="src">Blind Fable §1</span>.</li>
358	      <li><b>Blind Opus</b> put Fable on one implementer, the checker part where a wrong repair is least visible to tests, Opus on judgement, Sonnet on every launch, collection, gate and landing <span class="src">Blind Opus §1</span>.</li>
359	      <li>Both put Fable on implementers, where your rule pins workers to Opus, and Sonnet well beyond archaeology <span class="src">POSITIONS</span>. Section 5 gives each choice with its reasons.</li>
360	    </ul>
361	    <p>So the blind two show what each designer builds from the content alone, short of what CLAUDE.md lets through.</p>
