541	  <div class="sub" id="blind-fable">
542	    <h3>The blind Fable design</h3>
543	    <p class="before"><b>Shape:</b> four <i>chains</i>, one per piece, each in its own worktree on its own branch. A chain is a run of <i>sub-pieces</i>, each one implementer followed by a checker: nine in all, W one, C three, G two, N three. Why so fine: one context per piece "would pass 300,000 tokens"; nine stay near 150,000 to 250,000 <span class="src">Blind Fable §1</span>.</p>
544	    <p class="before"><b>Before the chains</b> a Sonnet base agent reuses the base build or builds it (about 200 s), seeds the four worktrees, and stops if the base's per-site list is missing <span class="src">Blind Fable §2</span>. <b>Order:</b> the chains run through the harness's <code>pipeline()</code>, so both slots stay busy, with no wait until all four end <span class="src">Blind Fable §1</span>.</p>
545	    <div class="picks">
546	      <p class="before"><b>Tiers, its own choice</b> <span class="src">Blind Fable §1</span>:</p>
547	      <ul>
548	        <li><span class="tier fable">Fable</span> for W, C-a and C-b, "the three agents whose edit restates a rule every test depends on".</li>
549	        <li><span class="tier opus">Opus</span> for C-c and the five library repairers, "whose work is judgment over much reading"; also the reviewers, the integrator and the report writer.</li>
550	        <li><span class="tier sonnet">Sonnet</span> "for everything mechanical: verification by checklist, running suites, reading measurements, the S1-form check".</li>
551	      </ul>
552	    </div>
553	    <ol class="roles">
554	      <li>
555	        <div class="role-head"><span class="role-name">Implementer</span><span class="tier fable">Fable ×3</span><span class="tier opus">Opus ×6</span><span class="count">×9</span></div>
556	        <p>Works in its chain's worktree after the sub-piece before it. Runs each test in the untouched worktree first and keeps the output as a record outside the repository; only then edits. W and C rebuild after their own edits (25 to 40 s); G and N build nothing. One commit: tests, fix and specification text. It runs no suite and no distance, and returns its ledger lines instead of editing the ledger <span class="src">Blind Fable §1, §2</span>.</p>
557	        <p class="cost">Estimate: <b>1.35M</b> on Fable, about 450K each; <b>2.4M</b> on Opus, about 400K each <span class="src">Blind Fable §4</span></p>
558	      </li>
559	      <li>
560	        <div class="role-head"><span class="role-name">Checklist verifier</span><span class="tier sonnet">Sonnet</span><span class="count">×9, about 4 more to re-verify</span></div>
561	        <p>Reads the diff and the records; runs nothing. Eight items: the paths touched, no team test changed, the test's failure seen in its record before the fix, each library hunk in its piece's declarations, no cast round the checker, the S1 form, the ledger lines, the report. "Cheap, mechanical, high yield" <span class="src">Blind Fable §2, §3</span>.</p>
562	        <p class="cost">Estimate: <b>0.9M</b>, 13 at about 70K <span class="src">Blind Fable §4</span></p>
563	      </li>
564	      <li>
565	        <div class="role-head"><span class="role-name">Semantic reviewer</span><span class="tier opus">Opus</span><span class="count">×3: W, C-a, C-b</span></div>
566	        <p>Reads the Java and Scala diff beside the passages, and asks whether the code states exactly the rule: not wider, not narrower. Builds and runs nothing. Only these three, because "a three-vote adversarial panel is not [the right price], because the suites are a stronger refuter than a second reader" <span class="src">Blind Fable §3</span>.</p>
567	        <p class="cost">Estimate: <b>0.4M</b>, about 130K each <span class="src">Blind Fable §4</span></p>
568	      </li>
569	      <li>
570	        <div class="role-head"><span class="role-name">Fixup</span><span class="tier none">the implementer's tier</span><span class="count">about 4, up to 2 more</span></div>
571	        <p>After a verifier's or a reviewer's issue: one more commit, test first. A re-verify follows a verifier's fixup. A library chain whose sites survive its distance run gets one more pass <span class="src">Blind Fable §2</span>.</p>
572	        <p class="cost">Estimate: <b>0.7M</b>, about 120K each, 2 on Fable and 4 on Opus <span class="src">Blind Fable §4</span></p>
573	      </li>
574	      <li>
575	        <div class="role-head"><span class="role-name">Gate runner</span><span class="tier sonnet">Sonnet, low effort</span><span class="count">×7</span></div>
576	        <p>At each chain's end: <code>testSystem</code> for all, <code>testFast</code> for C, the distance for C, G and N. Once on the merged tree: both suites and both measurements. Never builds. A lock keeps two suites from sharing the four cores <span class="src">Blind Fable §1, §2</span>.</p>
577	        <p class="cost">Estimate: <b>0.35M</b>, about 50K each <span class="src">Blind Fable §4</span></p>
